! mcp_bootstrap_p184.strap -- MCP printed page 184: the tail of the
! >>> CORRECTED 2026-11-02: THIS DECK READ THE FAMILY AS "ZIO". <<<
! It is "ZID".  The argument for ZIO was that the typeset manuals
! print ZIO seven times and ZID never -- but ALL SEVEN of those hits
! are the SUBSTRING inside ZIOCH (3) and ZIOR (4); there is no bare
! ZIO family in any manual.  A substring grep is not a witness.
! The ink settles it: printed p.179 line 44 prints "VF,Z ID 04" with
! the blank in, and at 600 dpi that glyph has a straight vertical
! left stem and squared corners against the closed round O of
! SOWNER one row below.  printed pp.179 and 180 spell it ZID in 74
! places between them and are right.
!
! HOW IT WAS FOUND: not by check_symbol_spellings, which is blind to
! this pair -- frozenset("DO") is not in its CONFUSABLE set -- but by
! the merged body image, where ZID09 (defined on p.180) and ZIO09
! (SYNned here) stayed two separate symbols and left ZIO09/ZIO09X
! unresolved behind the frontier.
! "wrong reel / reload" arm of the tape-label routine, and the four
! (A*)DD operator-message cards that end it.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.191.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LD000040   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  184".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 221214. <<<
! Six digits, clear of the dashed rule, printed after the word
! LOCATION at the right-hand end of the heading line.  That is LINE
! 1'S location word (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and
! line 1 here IS a located row -- the case the rule predicts to be
! numbered rather than blank.  Line 1's own LOCATION column reads
! 221214.00.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-SIX PRINTED LINES.  No comment card, no asterisk banner,
! no SLC, no SYN, no VF, NO RESERVATION OF ANY KIND.  The page is
! CONTIGUOUS from 221214.00 to 221260.77 -- thirty-seven words,
! no hole -- and then blank paper to the foot of the form.
!
!   lines  1-8    the scratch-tape test and the KRELOD entry:
!                 B,K SP TM / K(BU,30) against the reel number /
!                 BZAE to K RELOD, then the $W-or-$WEF test and
!                 the two branch-on-bit cards that decide whether
!                 the operator gets a second chance.
!   lines  9-17   set up the unload message (K UNL M, Z UNLO2,
!                 K UL MES) and flip the two commentator flags
!                 J IOBSY and J WAIT.
!   lines 18-26   the SIC/branch pairs that ask for the correct
!                 reel, wait for the channel, clear the control
!                 word and re-test.
!   lines 27-31   K BOUNC: the second-time-round arm.  It points
!                 $1 at K LETTR, expands the reel number into
!                 K DRAG as 8-bit BCD, and throws the problem
!                 program off at J GETOF.
!   lines 32-35   the four message cards -- the letter text, and
!                 the two blank fields K DRAG that line 29 fills.
!   line  36      R RDL, an index word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE SURVEY IS WRONG ABOUT BOTH ENDS OF THIS PAGE, AND THE
! >>> ".50" IT WAS DOUBTED FOR IS REAL. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! docs/mcp_page_map.json's cached survey gives first=221214.40,
! last=221260.CC and 30 rows.  Measured off this page's ink:
! FIRST IS 221214.00 (the survey lost line 1, exactly as brief
! rule 4 predicts), last is 221260.00, and there are THIRTY-SIX
! rows.  The caller was told to distrust the "221255.50" because
! .50 is not a half-word boundary.  IT IS NOT SUPPOSED TO BE.
! Lines 32-35 are (A*)DD character data at EIGHT BITS A CHARACTER,
! so their locations fall wherever the character count puts them.
! The whole chain closes, digit for digit, on the page's own
! printed locations:
!
!   221237.00 + 42 chars x 8 = 336 bits -> 221244.20   line 32->33
!   221244.20 + 35 chars x 8 = 280 bits -> 221250.50   line 33->34
!   221250.50 + 40 chars x 8 = 320 bits -> 221255.50   line 34->35
!   221255.50 + 19 chars x 8 = 152 bits -> 221260.00   line 35->36
!
! and 221260.00 is what line 36 PRINTS.  Four unknown blank counts,
! four printed locations, no slack.  Assembled: K LETTR lands at
! 221237.00, K DRAG at 221250.50 and R RDL at 221260.00.
!
! SECOND, INDEPENDENT READING OF THE SAME FOUR COUNTS: the "*"
! end-of-datum character's own PRINTED COLUMN.  The prefix
! "(A*)DD(BU)," is eleven characters, so the terminator must stand
! at column 11+n.  Measured numerically off the 800-dpi render
! (glyph runs, 80 px a character, column 0 taken from the "(" of
! line 33):
!
!     line 32   predicted 53   measured 52.98
!     line 33   predicted 46   measured 46.00
!     line 34   predicted 51   measured 51.08
!     line 35   predicted 30   measured 29.98
!
! Line 35 carries one more mark, a SPECK at column 53.3 with no
! glyph shape; it is dirt, and if it were a character the
! arithmetic above would not close.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN, BOTH WAYS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! BACKWARD.  This deck's SLC is NOT PRINTED; the page carries no
! SLC card at all, so the origin 221214.00 is asserted from the
! heading-row LOCATION field plus line 1's own LOCATION column.
! Two cross-checks, neither of them from this page:
!
!   * the volume OCR of printed p.183 (PDF p.190, a DIFFERENT
!     page) ends at 221207.00 and its line 40 prints
!     "BZAE,RV3 SCR" compiling 221214.76 -- which pins THIS page's
!     line 2 at 221214.40 from the previous page's ink.  Flagged
!     as a cross-check, not as settled: p.183's deck does not
!     exist yet and the reading above is an OCR.
!   * printed p.183 line 44 prints "BAE,K RELOD" compiling
!     221217.76, the same half word this page's line 3 prints for
!     the same symbol.
!
! FORWARD.  Line 36 is a FULL word (an XW) at 221260.00, so
! >>> THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT 221261.00. <<<
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> FALSIFIABLE PREDICTIONS FOR printed p.185 (PDF p.192) <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   (1) its first LOCATED row is 221261.00.  If it is not, one of
!       us is wrong.
!   (2) its first card serial is LD000071.  This page's serials
!       run LD000040..LD000070 with no gap.  WEAKER THAN (1), and
!       say so: the brief records two measured cases of a serial
!       that prints NO listing line, ED000049 and IH000041, BOTH
!       AT PAGE BREAKS, which is exactly here.  A missing LD000071
!       would not by itself falsify the join; a serial BELOW
!       LD000071 would.
!   (3) its heading-row LOCATION field is BLANK, because the
!       volume OCR shows p.185's lines 1-3 are an asterisk banner
!       ("***** I/O REJECT TEST *****") and its first located row
!       is line 4.  That is printed p.147's case exactly.
!   (4) p.185 DEFINES none of the six names this page defines.
!   If p.185's line 4 turns out to be a printed "SLC,$" at
!   221261.00, that is a free independent confirmation of the join
!   and it would close the backward join this deck had to assert.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! CARD SERIALS -- AND A CORRECTION TO BRIEF RULE 4
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The serial column reads LD000040 on line 1 and LD000070 on line
! 36, continuous, no gap, and the heading's own "CARD ID
! LD000040" agrees with line 1.  So nothing fell off either end.
!
! BUT COUNTING ROWS OFF THE SERIAL COLUMN GIVES 31 WHERE THE PAGE
! PRINTS 36.  Five serials print TWICE -- LD000051, 56, 57, 58,
! 59 -- and each pair is two HALF-WORD instructions punched on one
! card:
!
!   lines 12/13  LD000051   SIC,ZID09X ; B,ZID09
!   lines 18/19  LD000056   SIC,K NEW TP ; B,K RITE T
!   lines 20/21  LD000057   LVI,0,K SUPP ; SVA,0,K NEW TP
!   lines 22/23  LD000058   SIC,K UNR R ; B,K K LOOP
!   lines 24/25  LD000059   SIC,K CBJ R ; B,K CLEAR
!
! The serial column counts CARDS, and a card can print two lines.
! Four of the five pairs are SIC/branch pairs, which is the shape
! the workflow already knows; the fifth (LVI;SVA) is not, so the
! rule is "one card, one WORD", not "one card, one SIC pair".
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPH CALLS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) >>> ON THIS DRUM, NEITHER WIDTH NOR OPENNESS SEPARATES
!     LETTER O FROM DIGIT 0 FROM LETTER C.  MEASURED, SO THE NEXT
!     TRANSCRIBER NEED NOT SPEND THE HOUR. <<<  Run widths at 1600
!     dpi, segmented from the ink (not from a fixed cell), over
!     every instance on the page whose identity is known from its
!     English word:
!
!       letter O  124 120 104 112 108 120   (SOWNER JIOBSY LOOP
!                                            LOOP GETOF CNOP)
!       letter C  112 116 116 112 116 116   (SIC x4, CLEAR, CNOP)
!       digit 0   112 108 116 108 100 112   (LVI,0 ; SVA,0 ; 9.0 ;
!                                            $+2.0 ; (BU,40) ; the
!                                            0 of (BU,30))
!
!     The three ranges OVERLAP COMPLETELY.  Openness does not
!     separate them either: of six known letter O's four print
!     open like a C and two print closed, and of six known digit
!     0's four print closed and two open.  Template matching (IoU
!     on size-normalised bitmaps) returned 0.59 for the right
!     answer against 0.54 for the wrong one -- noise.  AN EARLIER
!     DRAFT OF THIS NOTE CLAIMED THE WIDTHS DID SEPARATE, on a
!     sample of two O's and one C; they do not.  What settled
!     every round glyph on this page was OTHER PAGES and the
!     MANUALS, never the shape.
!
! (2) ZID09 and ZID09X (lines 12, 13).  Settled, and not by ink:
!     * printed p.179 (PDF p.186, a DIFFERENT page) line 44 prints
!       the name with a BLANK IN IT -- "VF,Z ID 04" -- so the
!       family is Z + IO + a TWO-DIGIT number.  Its lines 45-48
!       print Z ID09A, ZID04A, ZID09X and Z ID09, and its line 47
!       is the SAME CARD as this page's line 12 (SIC,ZID09X,
!       compiling 221045.00 80) and its line 48 the same as this
!       page's line 13.
!     * the volume OCR carries ZID01 (221040.00), ZID12
!       (221044.40) and ZID15 as references, which is the same
!       two-digit family.
!     * grep over the TYPESET manuals: "ZIOCH" and "ZIOR" occur,
!       "ZID" occurs ZERO times in either revision of C22-6728.
!     So the characters are Z, I, letter O, digit 0, digit 9.
!
! (3) ZUNLO2 (line 11): THE ADDRESS IS SETTLED, THE SPELLING IS
!     NOT.  printed p.197 (PDF p.204) line 21 is the DEFINING card
!     -- "222011.00 *   Z UNLO2   (IQSX)DD(BU),      X" -- and
!     line 11 here compiles 222011.17 01, which is that address.
!     But the fifth character is the same O/0 question as
!     everywhere else, and I DID NOT SETTLE IT; see WHAT I COULD
!     NOT SETTLE (E).  A first draft of this note called it "a
!     wide closed O beside narrow digit 0s in its own location
!     column", which is a shape argument on a page I had not
!     calibrated -- exactly the move glyph call (1) says carries
!     no information, and the one GLYPH CALL (8) punished.
!
! (4) K UNL M (line 10) and K UL MES (line 15) are likewise read
!     off printed p.197, whose lines 19 and 26 are their defining
!     cards at 222001.00 and 222017.40 -- the two addresses this
!     page's LVI and LV compile.
!
! (5) The byte on line 29 prints "CC" to the eye and is D0.
!     Settled on what produced it: ST(BU,40) with no second
!     operand has i2 = 0, so the low nibble is 0, and the high
!     nibble is the STORE code this page prints cleanly on line
!     21 ("221766.01 D0", SVA).  printed p.175's deck records the
!     same D0-prints-as-DC for three ST rows.
!
! (6) Lines 3 and 5 print "CO" and are C0: a hex byte CAN hold C,
!     and the assembler produces C0 for BZAE and BZXE.  Not every
!     round glyph on this page is a damaged 0.
!
! (7) "FAKE EPGK" (line 5's comment) is read at 1600 dpi and is
!     recorded as printed; no manual spells it.
!
! (8) >>> I READ LINE 24 AS "K OBJ R" AND I WAS WRONG.  IT IS
!     "K CBJ R". <<<  This is worth the space because of HOW it
!     was caught and how it was settled.
!
!     English led me astray: "OBJ" is a word-shaped fragment and
!     "CBJ" is not, so rung 1 of the glyph ladder actively voted
!     for the wrong answer.  tests/check_symbol_spellings.py
!     caught it -- "two spellings of one symbol at 4764384: KCBJR
!     (pp.125,126) vs KOBJR (pp.184) -- glyph C/O" -- which is
!     exactly the defect that check exists for, and nothing else
!     in this repository could have seen it: the !CHECK on line
!     24 passes either way.
!
!     Settled TWICE, neither time off this page:
!       * printed p.186 (PDF p.193) line 9 is the DEFINING card,
!         "221313.40   221263.10 00   K CBJ R   B,K RETRY   -CTL
!         ORD CLEARED FOR RETRY", and its C is rendered here at
!         1600 dpi: narrow, wide gap, unmistakably a C.
!       * the TYPESET symbol index of C22-6728 AND of C22-6728-1
!         lists "KCBJ" -- and lists it ALPHABETICALLY BETWEEN the
!         J-names and KCLEAR.  The POSITION is the evidence and it
!         is immune to OCR: a KOBJ would sort after KNSYST and
!         before KOPOK, and neither index has anything there.
!
!     pp.125 and p.126 had it right, from the volume OCR, and this
!     page's transcriber had it wrong from the ink.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LISTING MARKS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every line number carries the ordinary trailing asterisk.
! THREE ROWS CARRY A SECOND ASTERISK AFTER THE LOCATION:
!
!     line 12   221223.00 *   SIC,ZID09X
!     line 31   221236.40 *   CNOP
!     line 34   221250.50 *   K DRAG  (A*)DD(BU),
!
! Recorded, no rule proposed -- and line 34 is a SHAPE NOT ON THE
! EXISTING LIST OF EIGHT: a located DD card carrying a NAME.  The
! three have nothing in common on this page: one is an SIC, one a
! CNOP, one a DD; two of the three are NOT label rows; the page's
! three OTHER SIC rows (18, 22 and 24) carry NO mark; and the
! other three (A*)DD rows (32, 33, 35) carry none either, so it
! is not "every DD" and not "every named row".  A page that would kill any rule
! built from these three is printed p.185, whose banner rows will
! show whether the mark can appear on a row that compiles
! nothing.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Twenty-two.  SEVEN WERE ALREADY LANDED BY OTHER PAGES and are
! copied in the form the defining page's deck uses, so the merged
! image cannot disagree:
!
!   K SP TM   220156.40   p.182-region; eleven decks SYN it
!   E MK 1A   221152.40   p.153 "E MK1A VF,(8)3000."
!   K ILEGL   220064.0    p.160 line 8's own label row
!   S OWNER   0.32        p.99  line 30, dds (BU,1)
!   S REMNT   3.61        p.99  line 16, dds (BU,1)
!   K SUPP    216242.0    p.128 line 3 "K SUPP NOP,K COMM R"
!   K CLEAR   221302.40   OCR'd defining card
!
! FIFTEEN ARE NEW, and every one of their values is READ OFF THIS
! PAGE'S OWN BINARY OUTPUT.  Four of the fifteen are read to the
! BIT and eleven only to the HALF WORD; which is which matters and
! is recorded here rather than left to a passing !CHECK:
!
! >>> BIT-EXACT FROM THIS PAGE, because the operand is a SIC or a
! >>> VFL ADDRESS <<<
!   Z ID09X   221045.00   line 12 SIC   (p.179 line 47, same card)
!   K NEW TP  221766.00   line 18 SIC   (p.196 line 42 defines it)
!   K UNR R   221325.40   line 22 SIC   (p.186 line 30 defines it)
!   K CBJ R   221313.40   line 24 SIC   (p.186 line  9 defines it)
!   J IOBSY   233666.04   line 16 LF(BU,1) -- see the note below
!   J WAIT    233666.00   line 17 SF(BU,1) -- see the note below
!
! >>> HALF WORD ONLY FROM THIS PAGE (workflow item 1) -- but SIX
! >>> OF THE NINE ARE PINNED BY ANOTHER PAGE'S DEFINING CARD,
! >>> which the volume OCR indexes <<<
!   K W LABL  217711.0    line  6 B    (p.155 prints 217711.00)
!   Z ID09    221042.0    line 13 B    (p.179 line 48, same card)
!   K RITE T  221743.00   line 19 B    (p.196 line 18 defines it:
!                                       "K RITE T LF(BU,30,6),
!                                       .34($8)" at 221743.00)
!   K K LOOP  221321.40   line 23 B    (p.186 line 24 defines it:
!                                       "K K LOOP LCI,1,K REP")
!   R RR      221165.40   line 26 B    (p.183 line 41 defines it)
!   J GETOF   221546.40   line 30 B    (p.193 line  7 defines it:
!                                       "J GETOF CM0000(BU,1),
!                                       S CK OP($10)")
!   K UNL M   222001.0    line 10 LVI  (p.197 line 19 defines it)
!   Z UNLO2   222011.0    line 11 LVI  (p.197 line 21 defines it)
!   K UL MES  222017.40   line 15 LV   (p.197 line 26 defines it)
!
! Every "p.NNN line N defines it" above is the volume OCR of a
! DIFFERENT page used as an INDEX, which is what the workflow says
! it is good for.  Where the deck still writes ".0" the bit stays
! this corpus's convention: an OCR of another page's LOCATION
! column corroborates, it does not replace a rendering.
!
! >>> A VFL OPERAND ADDRESS IS BIT-EXACT, AND THAT IS A SECOND
! >>> EXCEPTION TO "KNOWN ONLY TO THE HALF WORD". <<<
! docs/transcription_workflow.md's WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE item
! 1 names SIC as the exception.  THIS PAGE PROVES VFL IS ANOTHER,
! and it proves it with two rows of its own: line 29 is
! "ST(BU,40),K DRAG" and prints the address 221250.50, and K DRAG's
! OWN DEFINING ROW, line 34, prints 221250.50 in the LOCATION
! column.  Same page, two independent rows, agreeing on a .50 that
! is neither 0 nor 32.  So J IOBSY at 233666.04 and J WAIT at
! 233666.00 are READINGS, bit for bit, and not conventions.  (A
! workflow doc entry is owed here; this deck cannot write under
! docs/.)
!
! AND VOLUME 2 CORROBORATES BOTH VALUES FROM A DIFFERENT
! ASSEMBLY.  docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_250-445.txt prints, in the "J"
! commentator code that these two flags belong to:
!
!   233666.04 80 001000.36 F0   CM1111(BU,1),JIOBSY
!   233666.04 80 000000.74 01   JOTST BZB,JIOBSY,32(JXIN1)
!   233666.00 80 002000.00 F0   JCOMM CM0000(BU,2),JWAIT
!   233666.01 80 001000.36 F0   MCOMD CM1111(BU,1),JWAIT.1
!
! -- .04 for J IOBSY three times, .00 for J WAIT, and the last row
! is J WAIT PLUS ONE BIT printing .01, which only closes if J WAIT
! really is at bit 0.
!
! J IOBSY and J WAIT ARE SYNned WITH NO DATA DESCRIPTION, on
! purpose.  No page in the body range pp.99-184 DEFINES them --
! they live at 0o233666, in the region volume 2 of the listing
! (docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_250-445.txt) covers, where the OCR shows
! "CM1111(BU,1),JWAIT.1" and "BZB,JIOBSY,32(JXIN1)".  Every use on
! THIS page writes its own (BU,1), so nothing inherits from the
! symbol and nothing can be lost.  A later body page that SYNs
! them should write them the same way, or the merge will see one
! page saying (BU,1) and another saying nothing.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (A) THREE NAMES DEFINED HERE REST ON THIS PAGE'S INK AND ON
!     ENGLISH, AND ON NOTHING ELSE: K BOUNC (line 27), K LETTR
!     (line 32) and K DRAG (line 34).  No other page in either
!     volume's OCR prints any of them, and the K section of the
!     typeset index in BOTH revisions of C22-6728 begins at KCBJ
!     -- there is no KB entry at all -- so rung 2 of the ladder
!     comes back empty for all three.  What each rests on:
!       * K DRAG's D IS SETTLED, on the strongest rung there is:
!         the SAME ROW prints "(A*)DD(BU)," four characters later,
!         so the D of DRAG stands beside two known D's off the
!         same drum in the same line, and they match.
!       * K LETTR is "letter", and line 32 IS the letter.
!       * K BOUNC is the one I am least sure of.  Its two round
!         glyphs measure 124 and 116 px, which by glyph call (1)
!         means nothing, and "BOUNC" is a reading of English --
!         the comment four lines earlier is "-IF 2ND TIME THROW
!         HIM OFF".  I did NOT settle it, and after glyph call (8)
!         I distrust the English argument specifically.  WHAT
!         WOULD SETTLE IT: any other page referencing 221233.40,
!         or the C22-6728 index growing a KB entry in a cleaner
!         OCR.
!
!     K UNR R (line 22) WAS in this list and is no longer, and the
!     way out is worth recording because the MANUAL POINTED THE
!     WRONG WAY.  C22-6728 and C22-6728-1 both list "KUNR", four
!     letters, which looks like evidence that the fifth character
!     is mine.  IT IS NOT: printed p.186 prints BOTH symbols --
!     line 15 "K UNR  SIC,K CONV R" at 221314.00 and line 30
!     "K UNR R  B,..." at 221325.40 -- and its line 12 comment
!     spells the calling sequence "SIC,K UNR R ** B,K UNR".  Two
!     different routines; the index lists the other one.  Two more
!     pages of the listing spell this page's symbol closed up,
!     "SIC,KUNRR".
!
! (B) WHETHER THE BLANKS IN THE NAME FIELD ARE SIGNIFICANT.  Six
!     names are defined here and five print with a blank -- "RV3
!     SCR", "K BOUNC", "K LETTR", "K DRAG", "R RDL" -- while the
!     sixth, "KRELOD", prints CLOSED UP on line 7 although line 3
!     refers to it as "K RELOD".  One page prints the same symbol
!     both ways.  strap_asm cannot carry a blank in a label field
!     at all, so all six are written closed up, which is the
!     convention printed p.171's deck set for "K GOOF".  Nothing
!     here settles whether STRAP's own name field treated the
!     blank as data.
!
! (C) THE BRIEF'S OPEN QUESTION -- does a mode-only dds "(BU)"
!     inherit a LENGTH from its operand symbol? -- IS NOT
!     ANSWERED BY THIS PAGE, and the four "(A*)DD(BU)" rows are
!     not the case it needs: their operands are literal character
!     data, not symbols.  What they DO show is that "(BU)" is not
!     universally an effective length of 64 -- line 32's forty-two
!     characters compile 336 bits, not 64 -- because an entry-mode
!     DD takes its length from the datum.
!
! (D) "EPGK" in line 5's comment (GLYPH CALL 7).
!
! (E) >>> ZUNLO2 OR ZUNL02 (line 11).  OPEN. <<<  The VALUE is
!     settled -- 222011.00, printed p.197 line 21's own LOCATION
!     column -- and only the fifth character is in doubt.
!
!     FOR A LETTER O: the volume OCR reads "Z UNLO2" on p.197 and
!     "Z UNLO1" on p.196, and the Z-prefixed tape routines in the
!     typeset index are all letters (ZUNLDB, ZREWCD, ZREWST,
!     ZSTART, ZTCC).
!     FOR A DIGIT 0: the sister family on this page is numbered
!     with TWO-DIGIT suffixes -- Z ID01, Z ID09, Z IO12, Z IO15,
!     and p.179 prints one of them with the blank in, "Z ID 04" --
!     and Z UNL01/Z UNL02 would be the same convention.
!
!     MEASURED, AND IT DOES NOT HELP: printed p.197's drum does
!     not separate the glyphs either.  Run widths at 1600 dpi on
!     its line 19, which carries known letters and known digits in
!     one row: letter O ("ON") 128, letter C ("REPLACE", "CHANNEL")
!     124 and 112, digits ("64", "32", "8") 112, 112, 104, 116,
!     112.  The glyph in question measures 116, in the middle of
!     all of them.
!
!     THE DECK WRITES ZUNLO2, because that is what two pages'
!     OCR spells and what the next transcriber is likeliest to
!     write, and because the symbol is referenced here and defined
!     elsewhere so the merge will pair them only if we agree.
!     WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: printed p.197's transcriber rendering
!     the NAME field beside a known digit 0 IN THE SAME ROW -- its
!     line 21 statement "(IQSX)DD(BU)," has none, but its line 19
!     name "K UNL M" and line 20 name "KREELN" sit in the same
!     column and a third Z UNL card may carry one.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! JOINING IT TO THE BODY IMAGE -- MEASURED, AND ONE WARNING
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The join is tests/check_mcp_body_image.py's "FIRST, LAST" and its
! body_page_count, NOT strap/mcp_image.py's MCP_IPL_PAGES (p.169
! caught the workflow doc saying the wrong thing).  This deck
! cannot edit under tests/, so the merge was run by hand instead:
!
!   pp.99-176 + this page  ->  merges without disagreement,
!                              701 defined, 75 external,
!                              every_word_accounted_for = 0
!
! tests/check_symbol_spellings.py and
! tests/check_deck_listing_comments.py were run the same way, and
! AGAINST THE LANDED decks/ DIRECTORY, not against a partial copy
! -- 448 decks, 1476 symbol definitions, no symbol spelled two
! ways; 110/165 decks with a parseable listing block, 2736 checks
! compared, 0 mismatches.  (Reach matters here: an earlier run of
! the spelling check against a smaller directory reported only 200
! definitions, and that is the check that caught GLYPH CALL (8).)
!
! >>> THE WARNING, AND IT IS THE SAME ONE printed p.175's DECK
! >>> WROTE. <<<  That zero is NOT evidence that nothing is
! missing.  This deck's SLC is a FORWARD SLC, so strap_asm records
! a skip, and the skip was PRINTED from that build rather than
! computed in my head (a first draft of this note said "220650,
! 364 words" and both halves were wrong):
!
!   0o220700..0o221213   204 words
!
! The last word the merge holds before the gap is 0o220677, which
! is printed p.176's last word, so p.176 leaves its counter at
! 220700.00 and the 204 words are pp.177-183's.  THEY ARE NOT A
! HOLE, and the body image will stay green across a gap it should
! not be green across until those seven pages land.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ASSEMBLER: NOTHING REFUSED.  Every one of the thirty-six printed
! statements was handed to strap_asm IN ITS PRINTED FORM and
! accepted, including the two card-level entry-mode prefixes
! "(A*)", the index-named operands "($4)/($11)/($12)", the
! bare-decimal bit offset ".34" (which prints .42 octal, and the
! two rows that use it would BOTH fail together if that rule were
! wrong), and the XW count written "1.0".  The only departures
! from the page are the five NAME-field blanks of note (B).
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

KSPTM   SYN, (8)220156.40            ! landed: eleven decks; line 1 prints 220156.50
EMK1A   SYN, (8)221152.40            ! landed: p.153 "E MK1A VF,(8)3000."
KILEGL  SYN, (8)220064.0             ! landed: p.160 line 8's label row
SOWNER  SYN(BU,1), 0.32              ! landed: p.99 line 30
