!   line 36  221260.00   221260.00+ 000 000001 221260  R RDL XW,$,1.0,$
!
! Lines 32-35 print NOTHING in the BINARY OUTPUT column -- four
! character strings and no machine code -- so no !CHECK can test
! them directly.  LINE 36 IS THEIR CHECK: its location depends on
! all four character counts, so if any count is wrong the XW lands
! somewhere else and the check below fails loudly.
!

!SYM RV3SCR 221214.40
!SYM KRELOD 221217.40
!SYM KBOUNC 221233.40
!SYM KLETTR 221237.00
!SYM KDRAG  221250.50
!SYM RRDL   221260.00
!
!CHECK 221214.00 | 220156.50 00 | line 1 B,K SP TM -SPACE OVER TAPE MARK -- K SP TM is an external landed by other decks at 220156.40, and a plain B shows only bit 32 (0o40 OR 0o10 = 0o50), so this row checks the half word and the branch byte, not the bit
!CHECK 221214.40 | 000000.42 84 036040.21 10 | line 2 RV3 SCR K(BU,30),.34($4),64 -TEST IF SCRATCH TAPE IS WANTED -- THE ".34" IS BARE DECIMAL AND PRINTS 0o42, which is the workflow's bare-literal rule put to work; 84's low nibble is index $4; the second word carries length 30 DECIMAL (0o036 in 036040) and the offset 64 in IBM bits 44-50 (the 1 of 036040's low octal digit pair). Line 28 computes the same address a second time
!CHECK 221215.40 | 221217.76 C0 | line 3 BZAE,K RELOD -- K RELOD is THIS page's line 7 at 221217.40, so this is a real address check and not a tautology: 0o40 OR 0o36 = 0o76. printed p.183 line 44 prints the identical half word for the same symbol
!CHECK 221216.00 | 221152.41 04 | line 4 KVI,0,E MK 1A -IF SO, TEST IF $W OR $WEF OP -- E MK 1A is p.153's "E MK1A VF,(8)3000." at 221152.40; .41 is 0o40 OR 2J+1 = 1 for J=0, the same 2J+1 rule the five LVI rows on this page print
!CHECK 221216.40 | 220064.32 C0 | line 5 BZXE,K ILEGL -IF NOT, FAKE EPGK -- K ILEGL is p.160 line 8's label at 220064.00; 0o32 is the ZXE indicator with bit 32 CLEAR, which is what confirms the landed ".0"
!CHECK 221217.00 | 217711.10 00 | line 6 B,K W LABL -IF SO, REWRITE LABEL -- target+.10 on K W LABL's 217711.00; the name is confirmed in TYPESET prose, C22-6728 "The write label routine is entered at KWLABL"
!CHECK 221217.40 | 000000.40 8B 221221.74 02 | line 7 KRELOD BB,SOWNER($11),$+2.0 -- S OWNER is (BU,1) at 0.32 (p.99 line 30) and 0.32 DECIMAL is bit 32 = 0o40, which is the whole of the first address; 8B's low nibble is index $11; $ is this row's 221217.40, so $+2.0 is 221221.40 and prints 0o40 OR 0o34 = 0o74 with the BB byte 02
!CHECK 221220.40 | 000003.75 8C 221233.74 0E | line 8 BB1,SREMNT($12),KBOUNC -IF 2ND TIME THROW HIM OFF -- S REMNT is (BU,1) at 3.61 DECIMAL = word 3 bit 61 = 0o75, so the bit-address conversion is checked outright; 8C is index $12; K BOUNC is THIS page's line 27 at 221233.40, printing the same 0o74 as line 7 and so pinning the label
!CHECK 221221.40 | 000024.20 30 | line 9 LV,8,$4 -- the operand is the index register's own cell, not a symbol of this page; .20 is 2J = 16 for J=8, which is the even-J counterpart of the LVI rows' 2J+1
!CHECK 221222.00 | 222001.13 01 | line 10 LVI,5,KUNLM -- .13 is 2J+1 = 11 for J=5 with bit 32 CLEAR, so K UNL M is at 222001.00; printed p.197 line 19's defining card "K UNL M (IQSX)DD(BU,64),REPLACE TAPE ON CHANNEL..." prints 222001.00 independently
!CHECK 221222.40 | 222011.17 01 | line 11 LVI,$7,ZUNLO2 -- .17 is 2J+1 = 15 for J=7, bit 32 clear; printed p.197 line 21's defining card prints 222011.00 and spells the name with a letter O
!CHECK 221223.00 | 221045.00 80 | line 12 SIC,ZID09X -- AN SIC CARRIES ITS OPERAND'S FULL 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS (workflow), so this row READS Z ID09X at 221045.00 to the bit. printed p.179 line 47 is the SAME CARD and prints the same half word. This row carries the first location-column asterisk
!CHECK 221223.40 | 221042.10 00 | line 13 B,ZID09 -- target+.10; bit offset unobservable. printed p.179 line 48 is the same card
!CHECK 221224.00 | 000011.17 01 | line 14 LVI,$7,9.0 -- the same J=7 .17 as line 11 against a LITERAL 9.0 (0o11) instead of a symbol, so the two rows are each other's control on the 2J+1 field
!CHECK 221224.40 | 222017.44 30 | line 15 LV,$2,KULMES -- .44 is 0o40 OR 2J = 4 for J=2, so bit 32 IS SET and K UL MES is at 222017.40, not .00; printed p.197 line 26's defining card "K UL MES VF,K UNL M+.192" prints 222017.40
!CHECK 221225.00 | 233666.04 80 001000.06 70 | line 16 LF(BU,1),JIOBSY -- A VFL OPERAND ADDRESS IS THE FULL 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS (this page proves it on line 29/34), so J IOBSY is read at 233666.04 BIT-EXACT; the second word is the (BU,1) dds 0o001000 with offset 0 and the LF code 06 70. The low nibble of the byte is i2 and is 0 because there is no second operand
!CHECK 221226.00 | 233666.00 80 001000.12 F0 | line 17 SF(BU,1),JWAIT -- the same word and the same dds as line 16 with bit 0 instead of bit 4 and the SF code 12 F0; the two rows differ in exactly the two fields their operands differ in
!CHECK 221227.00 | 221766.00 80 | line 18 SIC,K NEW TP -- SIC, so 221766.00 is bit-exact; line 21's SVA prints the same address and is its control, and printed p.196 line 42 is the defining card "K NEW TP B,K SUPP" at 221766.00
!CHECK 221227.40 | 221743.10 00 | line 19 B,K RITE T -ASK FOR CORRECT REEL -- target+.10, so only bit 32 is checked here; the name is confirmed in TYPESET prose, C22-6728 "When the routine is entered at KRITET or KUNLD1", and printed p.196 line 18's defining card prints the label at 221743.00
!CHECK 221230.00 | 216242.01 01 | line 20 LVI,0,K SUPP -- .01 is 2J+1 = 1 for J=0 on K SUPP's landed 216242.00; eleven decks print this half word, so this row corroborates and K SUPP's bit offset stays unreadable
!CHECK 221230.40 | 221766.01 D0 | line 21 SVA,0,K NEW TP -- the SAME symbol as line 18 through a different operation: .01 for J=0 against the SIC's .00, and the byte D0 which is the one this page prints cleanly and which settles line 29's
!CHECK 221231.00 | 221325.40 80 | line 22 SIC,K UNR R -- SIC, so 221325.40 is bit-exact including the .40. printed p.186 line 30 is the defining card and p.186 line 12's comment spells the calling sequence "SIC,K UNR R ** B,K UNR", which is what distinguishes this symbol from the K UNR at 221314.00 that the typeset index lists
!CHECK 221231.40 | 221321.50 00 | line 23 B,K K LOOP -WAIT FOR CHAN SIGNAL -- 0o40 OR 0o10 = 0o50, so K K LOOP is at 221321.40; the name is confirmed in TYPESET prose, C22-6728 "the subroutines, KKLOOP, KRETRY", and printed p.186 line 24 is the defining card "K K LOOP LCI,1,K REP -WAIT FOR UNIT TO BECOME READY"
!CHECK 221232.00 | 221313.40 80 | line 24 SIC,K CBJ R -- SIC, bit-exact at 221313.40. THE SPELLING WAS WRONG IN THIS DECK'S FIRST DRAFT ("K OBJ R") and tests/check_symbol_spellings.py caught it against pp.125 and 126; see GLYPH CALL (8). This !CHECK passes either way, which is exactly why that test exists
!CHECK 221232.40 | 221302.50 00 | line 25 B,K CLEAR -CLEAR CONTROL WORD -- K CLEAR is landed at 221302.40 by an OCR'd defining card and this row's 0o50 confirms bit 32, which is the only part a B can show
!CHECK 221233.00 | 221165.50 00 | line 26 B,R RR -TEST IF CORRECT REEL -- R RR at 221165.40; printed p.183 line 41 is its defining card and p.183 line 2 prints "R VFY BB,SYSTM($12),R RR" compiling 221165.74, a second witness for the same half word
!CHECK 221233.40 | 221237.03 01 | line 27 K BOUNC LVI,1,K LETTR -- .03 is 2J+1 = 3 for J=1 with bit 32 CLEAR, so K LETTR is at 221237.00 -- which is THIS page's line 32, the first message card, and the start of the character chain that ends on line 36
!CHECK 221234.00 | 000000.42 84 036600.06 70 | line 28 LF(BU,30,6),.34($4) -EXPAND REEL NO TO 8 BIT BCD -- the SAME first half word as line 2, so the decimal-.34 reading is checked twice on one page; the second word differs from line 2's only where the operands differ (byte size 6 instead of offset 64, length 30 unchanged) and carries the same LF code 06 70 as line 16
!CHECK 221235.00 | 221250.50 80 050000.20 D0 | line 29 ST(BU,40),K DRAG -- >>> THIS ROW IS WHY A VFL ADDRESS IS BIT-EXACT: it prints 221250.50 and K DRAG's own defining row, line 34, prints 221250.50 in the LOCATION column. Two independent rows on one page agreeing on a .50. The dds is length 40 DECIMAL (0o050000) and the byte is D0; it prints "CC" to the eye and is settled on what produced it, see GLYPH CALL (5)
!CHECK 221236.00 | 221546.50 00 | line 30 B,J GETOF -THROW PP OFF -- J GETOF at 221546.40; the name appears in C22-6728's typeset symbol index and printed p.193 line 7 is the defining card, "J GETOF CM0000(BU,1),S CK OP($10)" at 221546.40
!CHECK 221236.40 | 000000.30 00 | line 31 CNOP -- the counter arrives at an ODD half word here, so this CNOP EMITS one (221236.40) and the next card starts at 221237.00. This row carries the second location-column asterisk
!CHECK 221260.00 | 221260.00+ 000 000001 221260 | line 36 R RDL XW,$,1.0,$ -- THE ".0" OF THE COUNT IS UNOBSERVABLE: measured, "XW,$,1,$" and "XW,$,1.0,$" compile the identical word, so the printed "000001" pins the count to 1 and says nothing about the form of the card. >>> THE PAGE'S LOAD-BEARING CHECK. Lines 32-35 print no binary, so nothing can test them directly; this XW's LOCATION is the sum of their four character counts (42+35+40+19 characters at 8 bits from 221237.00), and if any count is wrong the word lands elsewhere and this check fails. Both $ operands are this row's own 221260.00, so the value field and the refill address are the same number, which is what the printed "221260.00+ ... 221260" says
